\subsubsection{Cost and time distribution}

Figure~\ref{fig:w411_scaling} plots the measured wall time against the
number of primary basis functions. The left panel gives the total
runtime and the middle panel the cost of a single Fock build. Dividing
by the number of Fock builds removes the variation in how many SCF
iterations each molecule happened to need, so the middle panel isolates
the scaling of the integral kernels themselves. The fitted exponents
rise with the size of the basis, which reflects that the richer sets
place proportionally more of the work in the dense linear algebra steps
that scale most steeply. This holds on both machines: the two sets of
exponents agree, so the cost of the algorithm grows with problem size in
the same way on either vendor's hardware, and the machines differ only
by a prefactor.

The right panel of Figure~\ref{fig:w411_scaling} gives that prefactor
directly, as the per-molecule ratio of the two Fock-build times, and
Table~\ref{tab:w411_timing} lists the mean wall times per basis set
alongside their geometric mean ratio. All cross-machine timings are
restricted to the species that converged on both machines, so the
comparison is not skewed by a differing set of molecules.
\todonum{One or two sentences here once the LUMI run lands: which machine
is faster, by how much, and whether the gap widens with basis size.}

We deliberately do not use the number of quadrature points on the
horizontal axis. With uniform radial sizing the molecular grid contains
$n_\mathrm{atoms}\times n_\mathrm{rad}\times n_\mathrm{ang}$ points
independent of the basis set, so it takes only as many distinct values
as there are molecule sizes in the set and cannot resolve the cost
differences between the four bases.

