\section{Conclusion} \label{sec:conclusion}

In this work we outlined the theory necessary to construct
Hartree-Fock and Kohn-Sham matrices from atomic orbital basis sets.
We provided a detailed algorithms and their implementations in C++
used the Kokkos framework for portability to GPGPUs. We tested the
implementations on two supercomputing clusters, where we compiled the same
programs from the exact same source code. The first one being Lumi
(AMD GPUS) and second one being Roihu (Nvidia GPUs), showing the
hardware agnostic nature of the code.

The dense implementations leverage the GPUs computing power to the
fullest relying heavily on General Matrix Matrix multiplication
subroutines, which is reflected in the benchmarks. The independence
of the dense implementations from any evaluation of the basis
functions, except in the pre-computation step, which can be offloaded
to a caller program, offers great reuseability potential. The modular
nature of the implemented kernels make it easy to interface with
other reusable libraries such as \texttt{IntegratorXX} and
\texttt{OpenOrbitalOptimizer}.

